lifeos-nix patch f-incidents-retire — retire the INCIDENTS narrative surface

PROBLEM
  Doctrine routes a whole class of learning into hand-written failure narratives at
  MEMORY/LEARNING/INCIDENTS/INC-YYYYMMDD-<slug>.md, with rules citing them by ID. In
  practice the surface was declared and never populated: the directory did not exist on
  this install after four months of daily use, while its siblings REFLECTIONS/ and
  FAILURES/ filled up on their own.

  It is also redundant with machinery that already works. FAILURES/ holds 654 machine-
  captured sentiment.json records, and RecurrenceLedger.ts derives failure classes from
  the observability streams the gates already write — no new write paths, no human in the
  loop. The one argument for hand-written narratives (later pattern-mining) is therefore
  already covered for free.

  And the measured trend says the alternative works: low-rating captures fell 309 -> 187
  -> 117 -> 41 across 2026-05..08. Fixing in the moment, then compressing the rule, is
  what produced that curve; no narrative was written for any of it.

  Principal's call (2026-09-01): "ni mi zares všeč to da bi documentala failures ... no
  need for a book of shame or whatev ... mnogo bolj efficient je sproti fixat stvari."

FIX (4 files) — remove every route that sends writing INTO the surface
  SelfHealing.md        — drop the routing-table row for the INC narrative.
  Verification.md       — drop the header sentence pointing at INCIDENTS/ as the home of
                          incident narratives (the rules themselves stay incident-derived;
                          only the narrative-filing instruction goes).
  v8.20.2.md            — drop the `incident` route from the Learning Router in claim 12;
                          the remaining routes (knowledge/rule/gotcha/state/business) are
                          untouched, and `default SKIP` already covers the dropped case.
  algorithm-tab.ts      — drop the Pulse "Incidents" doc-map entry (it pointed at a
                          README that does not exist).

  NOT touched, deliberately: Containment.md keeps the INC-YYYYMMDD naming convention in
  its release-gate description (a structural pattern, harmless if unused), and
  ProseInCode.ts keeps allowing an INC-ID in a code comment. Neither is a write path, and
  both would need re-adding if the surface is ever revived. No file, hook or tool reads
  the directory, so nothing breaks by its absence.

VERIFY
  grep -rn "MEMORY/LEARNING/INCIDENTS" $out/share/lifeos/  -> only Containment.md +
  ProseInCode.ts remain; no routing-table row, no Learning Router entry, no Pulse tab.

